= Solution
{parent=iii}

No. Let $n=7$, $r=3$, take $\mathcal A=\{\{1,2,3\}\}$, and let
$$
\mathcal B=\{B\in[7]^{(3)}:B\cap\{1,2,3\}\ne\varnothing\}.
$$
The families are nonempty and cross-intersecting. There are
$$
|\mathcal B|=\binom73-\binom43=31
$$
members, so
$$
|\mathcal A|+|\mathcal B|=32
>30=2\binom62.
$$

= iv
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Fix a <prime number> $p>r$. Distinct members of an intersecting $r$-uniform family have intersection size in
$$
L=\{1,2,\ldots,r-1\}\subset\mathbb F_p,
$$
whereas every member has size $r\notin L$ modulo $p$. The <Frankl-Wilson theorem> with $|L|=r-1$ therefore gives
$$
|\mathcal A|\leq\binom n{r-1}.
$$
For fixed $r$,
$$
\binom n{r-1}
=\binom{n-1}{r-1}\frac n{n-r+1}
=\binom{n-1}{r-1}\left(1+O(n^{-1})\right).
$$
This is the asserted asymptotic weakening of the <Erdős-Ko-Rado theorem>.

